#. §0: `use fun a b => max a b + 1`
#: Game/Levels/Specification/L03_TooWeakASpec.lean:42
msgid "You have to *build* a counterexample function. Anything that overshoots will do — try\n"
"§0."
msgstr ""

#. §0: `max`
#. §1: `constructor`
#: Game/Levels/Specification/L03_TooWeakASpec.lean:45
msgid "Two things left: that your function really is an upper bound, and that it differs from\n"
"§0 somewhere. Split them with §1."
msgstr ""

#. §0: `a`
#. §1: `b`
#. §2: `intro a b`
#. §3: `∧`
#: Game/Levels/Specification/L03_TooWeakASpec.lean:48
msgid "Take arbitrary §0 and §1 with §2, then prove both halves of the §3."
msgstr ""

#. §0: `show a ≤ max a b + 1`
#. §1: `le_max_left`
#. §2: `omega`
#: Game/Levels/Specification/L03_TooWeakASpec.lean:51
msgid "§0 makes the goal readable, then §1 plus §2\n"
"finishes it."
msgstr ""

#. §0: `use 0, 0`
#: Game/Levels/Specification/L03_TooWeakASpec.lean:59
msgid "Now pick any concrete pair of numbers where the two disagree. §0 is as good\n"
"as any."
msgstr ""

#. §0: `fun a b => max a b + 1`
#. §1: `max`
#: Game/Levels/Specification/L03_TooWeakASpec.lean:65
msgid "§0 satisfies the proposed contract and is not §1. So the contract\n"
"does not say what its author meant.\n"
"\n"
"This is the everyday failure mode of specification: not a *false* statement, but a true one\n"
"that permits implementations you would reject. The repair is to say more — that the result is\n"
"one of the arguments, or that it is below every other upper bound — and the habit is to\n"
"challenge each contract by asking which wrong program could still satisfy it.\n"
"\n"
