#: Game/Levels/Specification/L07_TheDivisionContract.lean:10
msgid "The most famous contract in programming is the one for integer division, and it has two parts\n"
"that only mean something together:\n"
"\n"
"§0\n"
"\n"
"The first says nothing was lost. The second says the remainder was reduced as far as it goes.\n"
"Drop the first and §1 is fine; drop the second and §2 is fine. Neither\n"
"is division.\n"
"\n"
"§3 returns the pair §4. Prove it satisfies both clauses — under the\n"
"precondition §5, which you should expect by now to be load-bearing. (It is: Lean defines\n"
"§6, so the second clause fails outright at §7.)\n"
"\n"
"Two library facts do the work, and the contract is written in the direction they state it:\n"
"§8 is the first clause and\n"
"§9 is the second."
msgstr ""

#. §0: `divmod_correct : 0 < b → b * (divmod a b).1 + (divmod a b).2 = a ∧ (divmod a b).2 < b`
#: Game/Levels/Specification/L07_TheDivisionContract.lean:32
msgid "§0\n"
"\n"
"Quotient and remainder satisfy the two-part division contract whenever the divisor is\n"
"positive."
msgstr ""

#. §0: `divmod`
#: Game/Levels/Specification/L07_TheDivisionContract.lean:40
msgid "Prove that §0 meets the two-part division contract: nothing is lost, and the\n"
"remainder is smaller than the divisor."
msgstr ""

#. §0: `unfold divmod`
#. §1: `a / b`
