#. §0: `d0 ≈ n0`
#. §1: `IsSWBisimulation.isWeakBisimulation`
#. §2: `τ`
#. §3: `Divergence`
#. §4: `Termination`
#: Game/Levels/Refinement/L09_AStepNobodyCanSee.lean:90
msgid "The implementation and the specification are weakly bisimilar: §0, via\n"
"§1.\n"
"\n"
"Weak bisimilarity is what you usually want when comparing an implementation with a\n"
"specification, because specifications are written without the implementation's bookkeeping and\n"
"should not have to anticipate it. The cost of the weakening is real, though, and worth\n"
"knowing: weak bisimilarity cannot see how *much* internal work is done, so a system that\n"
"diverges — spinning on §2 forever and never producing the output — is weakly bisimilar to one\n"
"that produces it immediately. If termination matters, weak bisimilarity is not the relation\n"
"that establishes it, and CSlib's §3 and §4 are where to look instead.\n"
"\n"
"One relation left to discuss, which is the one you have been using all along without being\n"
"asked to choose it."
msgstr ""

#: Game/Levels/Refinement/L10_ChoosingTheRightRelation.lean:8
msgid "Choosing the Right Relation"
msgstr ""

#. §0: `l ~tr[stackImpl, counterSpec] n`
#. §1: `IsBisimulation.traceEq`
#. §2: `chooseLate`
#. §3: `chooseEarly`
#. §4: `IsBisimulation.isSimulation`
#: Game/Levels/Refinement/L10_ChoosingTheRightRelation.lean:10
msgid "Every level so far told you which relation to prove. This one does not; it tells you what you\n"
"need to know about two pairs of systems, and leaves you to notice that the answers come from\n"
"different places.\n"
"\n"
"**First.** A caller of the stack wants to know it cannot tell the stack from the counter —\n"
"that they are **trace equivalent**, §0. You could try to compute\n"
"both trace sets and compare them. You should not: you proved a bisimulation in Level 8, and\n"
"§1 says bisimilar states have the same traces. Picking the strong\n"
"relation first made the weak one free.\n"
"\n"
"**Second.** Someone proposes replacing §2 with §3, on the grounds that\n"
"they have the same traces — which they do. Show that they are nevertheless **not bisimilar**.\n"
"You do not need a new argument: a bisimulation is in particular a simulation, via\n"
"§4, and Level 7 showed there is no simulation in that direction.\n"
"\n"
"Together these are the shape of the whole world. Trace equivalence is what an observer who\n"
"only watches can detect. Bisimilarity is what an observer who interacts can detect. They are\n"
"different, bisimilarity is strictly stronger, and which one you need is a question about your\n"
"setting — not about Lean."
msgstr ""

#. §0: `relation_choice`
#: Game/Levels/Refinement/L10_ChoosingTheRightRelation.lean:33
msgid "§0\n"
"\n"
"Bisimilarity gives trace equivalence for free; trace equivalence does not give bisimilarity."
msgstr ""

#: Game/Levels/Refinement/L10_ChoosingTheRightRelation.lean:40
msgid "Establish both halves of the comparison: the stack and the counter are trace equivalent,\n"
"and the two choice systems are not bisimilar even though they are."
msgstr ""

#. §0: `constructor`
#: Game/Levels/Refinement/L10_ChoosingTheRightRelation.lean:46
msgid "Two independent claims. §0 splits them."
msgstr ""

#. §0: `IsBisimulation.traceEq`
#. §1: `intro l n h`
#. §2: `exact stack_bisim_counter.traceEq h`
#: Game/Levels/Refinement/L10_ChoosingTheRightRelation.lean:48
msgid "Introduce the states and the relating equation, then hand the bisimulation to\n"
"§0: §1, then §2."
msgstr ""

#. §0: `rintro ⟨r, hr, hb⟩`
#: Game/Levels/Refinement/L10_ChoosingTheRightRelation.lean:52
msgid "Assume a bisimulation and take it apart: §0."
msgstr ""

#. §0: `exact late_not_sim_early ⟨r, hr, hb.isSimulation⟩`
#: Game/Levels/Refinement/L10_ChoosingTheRightRelation.lean:54
msgid "A bisimulation is a simulation, and Level 7 ruled those out:\n"
"§0."
msgstr ""

#: Game/Levels/Refinement/L10_ChoosingTheRightRelation.lean:58
msgid "Four relations, and the discipline for choosing between them.\n"
"\n"
"* **Conditional functional equivalence.** The replacement computes the same answers, on the\n"
"  inputs where the original was meaningful. Say which inputs.\n"
"* **Trace inclusion — refinement.** Every behavior of the implementation is permitted by the\n"
"  specification. One-directional, and that asymmetry is a feature.\n"
"* **Simulation.** A local condition implying trace inclusion, and strictly stronger than it.\n"
"  A simulation one way says nothing about the other.\n"
"* **Bisimulation.** One relation answering challenges from both sides. Implies trace\n"
"  equivalence, and is implied by it only under extra assumptions such as determinism. Weakened\n"
"  to ignore internal steps, it is the usual notion of *this implementation realises this\n"
"  specification*.\n"
"\n"
"The habit to carry away is to ask what an observer of your system can actually do before\n"
"choosing. If they only read a log, trace equivalence is what you need and anything stronger is\n"
"work you did not have to do. If they can interact — hold a handle, make a request and insist\n"
"on a response — trace equivalence is not enough, and proving it would be proving something true\n"
"that does not mean what you wanted.\n"
"\n"
"And the caveat from Level 5 one last time, because it is the easiest thing in this world to\n"
"overclaim. Everything here is about the Lean models on this page. A relation between two\n"
"models is evidence about two programs only to the extent that the models are faithful, and\n"
"establishing *that* is a different kind of work than anything this game has asked you to do."
msgstr ""

#: Game/Levels/Refinement.lean:4
msgid "Refinement and Behavioral Equivalence"
msgstr ""

#: Game/Levels/Refinement.lean:6
msgid "*Optional branch.* You can finish the core path without this world, and nothing later depends\n"
"on it. It is here because the question it asks comes up constantly in practice: **may I\n"
"replace this component with that one?**\n"
"\n"
"Sometimes the honest answer is *yes, they compute the same thing* — but only on inputs that\n"
"satisfy some condition. Sometimes it is *the replacement is safer, and that is a different\n"
"claim from behaving the same*. Sometimes the two systems do not even have the same notion of\n"
"state, and the only thing you can compare is what an outside observer sees. And sometimes the\n"
"implementation does internal work that no observer can see at all, and the right notion of\n"
"equivalence has to ignore it.\n"
"\n"
"So this world is about choosing the right relation: conditional equivalence, trace inclusion,\n"
"one-way simulation, bisimulation, and weak bisimulation. They are not interchangeable, and\n"
"picking the wrong one is how people end up proving something true that does not mean what they\n"
"wanted. You will see a pair of systems that are related in one direction and provably not in\n"
"the other, and a pair that offer an observer identical behavior and are still not\n"
"interchangeable.\n"
"\n"
"A caution before you start. These are statements about the Lean models written here, and about\n"
"nothing else. A proof that this list-based stack refines this counter is not a proof about any\n"
"C or Rust program, and a model in which a bad operation simply has no transition says nothing\n"
"about what a real language does when a program performs one."
msgstr ""

#: Game.lean:10
msgid "Introduction to Program Verification"
msgstr ""

#. §0: `intro`
#. §1: `exact`
#. §2: `rw`
#. §3: `simp`
#: Game.lean:12
msgid "Most programmers test. This game is about the other thing you can do: **prove**.\n"
"\n"
"You will start by describing what a program *does* — states, transitions, runs — then learn to\n"
"say what it *should* do, then prove that it does. The examples stay small on purpose; the\n"
"arguments are the point, and the arguments are the ones that scale.\n"
"\n"
"Along the way you will use [CSlib](https://github.com/leanprover/cslib), Lean's library of\n"
"computer-science abstractions. You will work with automata and labelled transition systems,\n"
"apply theorems to prove invariants, verify algorithm correctness, and explore simulation and\n"
"refinement. Part of the lesson is *why* a library like that is worth having — twice in the core\n"
"path you will get a theorem for nothing that would otherwise have cost you an induction.\n"
"\n"
"**The core path** is Worlds 1 to 4. **World 5 is optional**: it opens once you have met\n"
"transition systems in World 3, and asks when one implementation may replace another.\n"
"\n"
"No prior formal-methods, automata or CSlib knowledge is assumed. Some basic Lean tactic\n"
"experience will help: §0, §1, §2, §3, and elementary induction."
msgstr ""

